<Project>

  <!--
    PIN THE SDK'S IMPLICIT ILLink PACKAGE, SO A RESTORE IS A PROPERTY OF THE REPO AND NOT OF THE BOX.

    Three projects set IsAotCompatible=true (Cli, TestKit, Telemetry). That turns on the trim/AOT
    analyzers, and the SDK responds by adding an IMPLICIT PackageReference to
    Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks whose version comes from the installed SDK's KnownILLinkPack
    (in that SDK's own Microsoft.NETCoreSdk.BundledVersions.props) and NOT from
    Directory.Packages.props. Central package management does not manage implicit references, so
    there is no PackageVersion that can hold this down.

    The reference is Direct, so it lands in packages.lock.json, and its version moves with whatever
    SDK the machine happens to have: global.json pins 10.0.100 with rollForward=latestFeature and CI
    asks setup-dotnet for '10.0.x', so "whatever SDK" genuinely differs between contributors and
    between CI runs. main was carrying 10.0.3 in src/BattleScribeSpec.Cli's lock and 10.0.10 in the
    other two, written by different machines, and a plain `dotnet restore` rewrote whichever ones
    disagreed with the local SDK. Four separate branches hit that churn and reverted it as unrelated
    noise before anyone traced it here.

    Pinning ends that, and it is what makes locked-mode restore (enforced by the "Verify lock files"
    step in .github/workflows/ci.yml) mean anything at all: without it, CI's floating '10.0.x' would
    fail the repo on the next SDK patch. The pack is a build-time trimmer/analyzer with
    PrivateAssets=all: it ships in nothing, so pinning it a patch behind the SDK's bundled copy costs
    only newer analyzer diagnostics.

    Bumping is deliberate and manual, because dependabot does not see this item: read the net10.0
    ILLinkPackVersion out of the SDK's BundledVersions.props, edit the line below, then re-restore
    with the force-evaluate switch.

    This MUST live in Directory.Build.targets, not Directory.Build.props: KnownILLinkPack is defined
    by Microsoft.NET.Sdk.DefaultItems.props, which the SDK imports AFTER Directory.Build.props, so an
    Update there silently does nothing (measured: the restore still picked the SDK's version).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <KnownILLinkPack Update="Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks" ILLinkPackVersion="10.0.10" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
